# tools/ales_stereo_compress_witness.rish — stereo_compress: the compressor carried into stereo, one LINKED GAIN from the max detector, the image held to the sample (ALES157).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES157.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-062700_fill-ales157-lotus-stereo-compress.md
# Stands on: lotus/compress.rye (ALES50 — the mono compressor, its compress and CompressError reused whole for the loud-channel proof and the fault set), lotus/stereo_gate.rye (ALES156 — the sibling that opened the stereo
# dynamics family with the linked-detector law this rung deepens into a linked-gain law), lotus/stereo_normalize.rye (ALES129 — the amplitude-class one-shared-gain this rung makes dynamic and per-pair), lotus/pan.rye
# (ALES10 — the StereoClip and its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip, sample_max).
#
# lotus/stereo_compress.rye compresses a span of sample-PAIRS in a master above a magnitude threshold by a ratio, with ONE linked gain across the two channels. The detector key per pair is max(|left|, |right|): within the
# threshold the pair passes byte for byte; above it the compressed master new_key = threshold + (key − threshold)·den/num is computed once (exactly the mono compress of the loud channel) and BOTH channels are scaled by
# the single fraction new_key/key. The gate's below-threshold factor is constant, but the compressor's gain depends nonlinearly on the magnitude, so compressing each channel by its own magnitude would pull the loud
# channel down harder than the quiet one and shift the image — the dynamic, per-sample twin of the bug ALES129 named for amplitude. CompressError reused whole; the linked lift adds no fault. Values only, never a length.
# The crux is the linked-gain law (the loud channel equals the mono compress byte for byte, the quiet channel scaled by the same fraction, the independent lift genuinely differing), the image law (the shared gain preserves
# the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase pair returning still out of phase), the sub-threshold law (a pair within the linked threshold passes byte for byte), and the knob laws (unit ratio the identity, a higher ratio harder, a
# huge ratio seating the linked key at the threshold — the limiter within).
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_compress_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-compress: Language EN — stereo_compress compresses a span of sample-pairs in a stereo master above a magnitude threshold by a ratio, with one linked gain across the two channels. For each pair the detector key is the max of the two channels' magnitudes, the louder of the two: within the threshold the pair passes byte for byte; above it the compressed master new_key equals threshold plus the excess (key minus threshold) times ratio_den over ratio_num, computed once exactly as the mono compressor would for the loud channel, and both channels are scaled by the single fraction new_key over key. The channel holding the peak lands on new_key, the quieter channel scaled by the identical fraction, so the left-to-right ratio is preserved. It validates threshold, ratio, and span once against the shared length before any write, so a refusal never compresses one channel and leaves the other torn. It writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced; an empty span is a clean no-op on both."
say "ales-stereo-compress: Style Radiant — ALES50 named the mono compressor: a sample within a magnitude threshold passes byte for byte, a sample above it has its excess divided by a ratio and the sample rewritten sign(x) times threshold plus the compressed excess, the limiter's ceiling made gentle, the most-reached-for dynamics tool that evens a vocal and glues a bus. Carried into stereo it carries ALES156's linking one honest step deeper. The gate's below-threshold attenuation is a constant factor, the same for every magnitude, so gating each channel by its own magnitude already applied the same factor to both — the link only decided whether to gate. The compressor is different: its gain factor depends nonlinearly on the magnitude, so compressing each channel by its own magnitude would pull the loud channel down harder than the quiet one, and the inter-channel level difference that is the stereo image would shift. This is the same trap ALES129 named for the static amplitude class, now dynamic and per-sample. So this rung computes one gain from the linked detector and applies it to both — the per-pair dynamic twin of the one-shared-gain. The crux is five laws: the linked-gain law (both channels scaled by the same fraction from the linked max, so the loud channel equals the mono compress byte for byte while the quiet channel is scaled by the identical fraction, the independent lift genuinely differing); the image law (the shared gain preserves the left-to-right ratio, an out-of-phase pair returns still out of phase, no magnitude expands and no sign flips); the sub-threshold law (a pair within the linked threshold passes byte for byte on both); the knob laws (unit ratio the identity everywhere, a higher ratio compressing at least as hard, a ratio far past the excess seating the linked key at the threshold — the loud channel on threshold, the quiet proportionally, the limiter within); and the balance, atomicity, and degenerate law (length held, each refusal leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count zero and the empty span the identity on both)."
say "ales-stereo-compress: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) compressed by one linked gain, one comparison of the linked key and at most one multiply-then-divide per channel per sample (computed in i64, the product of a magnitude and new_key at most 32768 times 32768 never overflowing), memoryless (no attack, release, or knee; those are later rungs); the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a compressor writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); threshold, ratio, and span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never compresses one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoCompressError is ALES50's CompressError (BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadRange) reused whole, the linked lift adding no fault. Nothing on the audio path can overflow: new_key is at most key, so every scaled magnitude is at most its input's, which already fits i16. The linked-gain law is proven against a master where the left always holds the peak — the left equals the mono compress of it byte for byte (8000 to 2750, 6000 to 2250, 500 within threshold, 9000 to 3000) while the right is scaled by the same fraction (2000 to 687, 1000 to 375, 100 within, 3000 to 1000), and the independent lift (mono compress of the right alone, 2000 to 1250) is shown to genuinely differ; the image law proves no magnitude expands and no sign flips over a mixed master and that an out-of-phase equal-magnitude pair returns still exactly out of phase; the sub-threshold law proves a master within the linked threshold passes byte for byte on both; the knob laws prove unit ratio the identity everywhere, a higher ratio monotone, and a huge ratio seating the linked key at the threshold (the loud channel on 1000, the quiet at 250 proportionally); and the balance, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove the length held, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the threshold a magnitude in sample units and the ratio a plain fraction, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_compress"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_compress.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-compress: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_compress.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-compress: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-compress: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-compress' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-compress: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-compress line"
